---
import PageLayout from '../../layouts/PageLayout.astro';
import LegalIdentity from '../../components/LegalIdentity.astro';
import { route } from '../../i18n/routes';
import { AUTHOR_NAME, AUTHOR_DESC, CONTACT_EMAIL } from '../../data/site-config';
import { KIT as K } from '../../lib/kit';
import { profilePageSchema, breadcrumbSchema } from '../../lib/seo';
const lang = 'en';
const path = route('about', lang);
const title = 'Visa calculators 2026: who publishes UK VisaCalc and why';
const description = 'UK VisaCalc is published by Radif Partners: free UK visa calculators built on the Home Office fee table, Immigration Rules and sponsor register. No account.';
---
<PageLayout title={title} description={description} lang={lang} jsonLd={[profilePageSchema({ path, lang }), breadcrumbSchema([{ name: 'Home', path: route('home', lang) }, { name: 'About', path }])]} breadcrumbs={[{ label: 'About' }]}>
  <article class="prose prose-slate max-w-none prose-headings:font-serif">
    <h1>About UK VisaCalc</h1>
    <p>{AUTHOR_DESC.en}</p>
    <h2>Why this site exists</h2>
    <p>Ask "how much will my visa cost" or "does my salary qualify" and the answers are spread across GOV.UK pages that each give one piece, tables of several hundred lines, law firm articles that end with a phone number, and a few calculators whose fees are a table or two out of date. UK VisaCalc does what the first Google result leaves to you: it crosses your occupation code, salary, hours and situation to give the exact Skilled Worker threshold and the gap in pounds, prices a visa for the whole family on the fee table of {K.date(K.FEE_DATE)}, and dates your ILR and citizenship with the rolling 180-day rule, where other calculators show stale fees or ask for your phone number.</p>
    <h2>Who publishes it</h2>
    <p>The site is published by {AUTHOR_NAME}, a company that builds free calculators and reference pages on public data. It is <strong>not legal advice</strong> and is <strong>not affiliated with the Home Office</strong>, UK Visas and Immigration or any government body. It sells no consultation, collects no contact details for law firms and receives nothing from employers or advisers.</p>
    <LegalIdentity lang={lang} />
    <h2>What we use</h2>
    <p>The Home Office fee tables ({K.FEES.versions.length} versions, from {K.date(K.FEES.versions[0].date)} to {K.date(K.FEE_DATE)}), the Immigration Rules (Appendix Skilled Worker, Skilled Occupations, Immigration Salary List, FM, Continuous Residence) as amended by Statement of Changes HC 584, the GOV.UK guidance pages of each route, the UKVI processing times of 9 September 2026 and the register of licensed sponsors of {K.date(K.SPONSORS.date)}. All are public sector information reused under the Open Government Licence v3.0. Nothing is taken from another visa website.</p>
    <h2>How it is checked</h2>
    <p>Each calculation is tested against the worked examples GOV.UK prints (a two-year Skilled Worker visa from Argentina at £2,889, the family surcharge for 2 years and 9 months, the citizenship example of the trip in June 2017) and against cases worked by hand from the rules. The <a href={route('method', lang)}>method page</a> lists the rules applied and their limits; corrections follow the <a href={route('editorial', lang)}>editorial policy</a>.</p>
    <h2>Contact</h2>
    <p>Corrections and questions about a figure: <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a>, or the <a href={route('contact', lang)}>contact page</a>. We cannot advise on an individual application.</p>
  </article>
</PageLayout>
